<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nativity scene</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as manger scene, crib, crèche, presepio, presepe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A nativity scene, also called a manger scene, crib or crèche, is a display, particularly during Christmastide, of figures representing the birth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. It shows Jesus, Mary and Joseph, usually with shepherds, angels, an ox and a donkey, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magi">Magi</a>. The tradition comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>: the first seasonal scene, which seems to have been enacted rather than sculpted, is attributed to Francis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assisi">Assisi</a>, who is said to have staged it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> 1223 in a cave near Greccio. Around 1290 Arnolfo di Cambio made a set of stone figures for Santa Maria Maggiore in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and in the early modern period sculpted cribs, often exported from Italy, were set up in many churches and homes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nativity-scene.jpg" alt="Nativity scene" width="330" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yvonne Bentele · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2005-12_Weihnachtskrippe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the thirteenth century popular Christmas songs in the vernacular had developed in France, Germany and particularly Italy, under the influence of Francis of Assisi, to whom the first <b>nativity scene</b> is also attributed: he is said to have staged it at Christmas 1223 in a cave near Greccio.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>The first <b>nativity scene</b>, which seems to have been enacted rather than sculpted, is attributed to Francis of Assisi, who is said to have staged it at Christmas 1223 in a cave near Greccio.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li></ul></section>
